,title,link,text,language,family
0,Shanenawa language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shanenawa_language,"January 1, 2026 - Shaninawa (Xaninaua) is a moribund Panoan language of Brazil, spoken by just 23 people in 2016. The extant Yawanawa language goes by the name Shaninawa/Shanenawa.",Shanenawa,Panoan
1,Panoan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Panoano,"2 weeks ago - In the list of Panoan languages below adapted from Fleck (2013), (†) means extinct, and (*) obsolescent (no longer spoken daily). Dialects are listed in parentheses. ... Yaminawa (Brazilian Yaminawa dialects, Peruvian Yaminawa, Chaninawa, Chitonawa, Mastanawa, Parkenawa (= Yora), Shanenawa, ...",Shanenawa,Panoan
2,Amahuaca language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amawaka_language,"May 28, 2026 - Amahuaca is a member of the Panoan language family. David Fleck (2013) groups Amahuaca as being closest to the extinct Remo language of the Môa river and Tuxinawa, and the still-spoken Kashinawa and Yaminawa dialect complex, which also includes Sharanawa, Shanenawa, Yora, and Yawanawa.",Shanenawa,Panoan
3,"Tribe Shanenawa - Katukina - Tribal Rapé, Mapacho, Kambo, Sananga, Shamanic Tools & Incenses",https://katukina.com/doc/shanenawa,"Their efforts to revitalize their language, music, and spiritual practices are a testament to their resilience. Today, the Shanenawa continue to use their sacred rituals and knowledge of the forest as a means of survival and cultural preservation.",Shanenawa,Panoan
4,Pajé Maná Shanenawá on Apple Music,https://music.apple.com/ie/artist/pajé-maná-shanenawá/1793701214,"February 14, 2025 - Listen to music by Pajé Maná Shanenawá on Apple Music.",Shanenawa,Panoan
5,[Digital] Sacred Chants & Ancestral Wisdom of the Shanenawa,https://digital.aniwa.co/courses/chants-shanenawa,Sacred Chants: Learn two Shanenawa chants and discover the profound significance of these sacred songs.,Shanenawa,Panoan
